# ANCHORED to the repo root -- an unanchored `build` ignores a directory of
# that name at ANY depth. The vendored trees below still need theirs ignored
# by name; they are stale CMake/autotools output (1347 files) that the
# codec scripts no longer create, since they build under $MT_OUT/_deps/work.
/build/
other/lib/**/build/
*.mode1v3
*.mode2v3
*.pbxuser
*.perspectivev3
*~.nib
# The ROOT .gitattributes is deliberate Git configuration, not an editor
# dropping: it pins LF endings for the commit-msg hook so Git for Windows
# cannot rewrite its shebang into "#!/bin/sh\r". A vendored tree's own copy
# stays ignored; this one is ours.
!/.gitattributes
.vs
.DS_Store
Browse.VC.db
log/
_RUNTIME_/log/
_RUNTIME_/Documents/deploy/
DEADJOE
UserInterfaceState.xcuserstate
*.so
*.a
*.ncb
platform/MacOS/MTEngineSDL.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/mars.xcuserdatad/xcdebugger/
platform/MacOS/MTEngineSDL.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/xcshareddata/
platform/MacOS/MTEngineSDL.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/xcshareddata/MTEngineSDL.xccheckout
platform/MacOS/build
platform/MacOS/libs/*.stamp
platform/MacOS/MTEngineSDL/.idea/
platform/Windows/.vs/
platform/Windows/bin/
platform/Windows/MTEngineSDL/x64/
platform/Windows/MTEngineSDL/Win32/
other/tools/DeployMaker/Debug/
other/tools/DeployMaker/Release/
bmfont2slrfont/Debug/
Debug/
# CORE DUMPS, and nothing else that happens to be spelled "core".
#
# This was `core.*` plus a bare `core`, which is a name rule pretending to be
# a content rule. It matched other/lib/libuv/src/{unix,win}/core.c -- libuv's
# event loop -- and, because macOS's filesystem is case-insensitive, the
# engine's own src/Engine/Core/ directory and every SDL src/core/. Six
# negation lines existed solely to undo it, one of them added by 33a4404
# after the same rule swallowed SDL2's src/core/. Tracked files stay tracked,
# so this repository never broke; the PUBLIC MIRROR did, silently, because
# its export rebuilt the tree with `git add -A` and both libuv core.c files
# were simply never added.
#
# A dump is `core.<pid>` (Linux) or lands in /cores (macOS). Neither shape
# can be confused with a source file or a directory, so the negations are
# gone with the rule that needed them.
core.[0-9]*
/cores/
*~
/Release/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/Win32/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/x64/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/SDL/Win32/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/SDL/x64/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/SDLmain/Win32/
other/lib/SDL2-2.0.10-static/VisualC/SDLmain/x64/
other/lib/uSockets/*.o
other/lib/mbedtls.macos/
other/lib/mbedtls.linux/
other/lib/mbedtls.windows-*/
# image-codecs/ holds downloaded upstream trees and build output -- ignored --
# EXCEPT patches/, which is code WE wrote: the build script calls it, and
# LibRaw's CDDL 1.0 requires our modification to be published. Note the trailing
# /* rather than /: git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is
# excluded, so the directory's contents are excluded and patches/ re-included,
# not the directory itself. Ignored, the patcher would silently not ship and a
# fresh clone would fail to build.
other/lib/image-codecs/*
!other/lib/image-codecs/patches/
other/lib/video-codecs/

# Visual Studio build outputs inside the vendored SDL2 tree
other/lib/SDL-release-2.32.10-static/VisualC/ARM64/
other/lib/SDL-release-2.32.10-static/VisualC/x64/
other/lib/SDL-release-2.32.10-static/VisualC/Win32/

# CMake build dir for the macOS static SDL2 build (platform/MacOS/build-sdl2.sh)
other/lib/SDL-release-2.32.10-static/build-macos/
other/lib/SDL-release-3.4.14-static/build-macos/

.superpowers/

# S-3 spike binaries (build with spikes/edr/build.sh; sources are tracked)
spikes/edr/edr_spike
spikes/edr/edr_probe
spikes/edr/edr_colorspace
tools/edr_abovewhite
spikes/edr/*.log
# EDR/HDR spike output written next to the tools that produce it (S-3/S-4).
tools/*.log
other/lib/SDL-release-3.4.14-static/build-linux/

# Generated by build-llama_cpp.sh into $MT_OUT/include/. It was TRACKED and
# rewritten by every build -- an app build modifying a tracked engine file.
src/Engine/Sci/Llama/llama_cpp_version.h

# Written by the Xcode capability pre-action. It normally lives BESIDE the
# repos, not inside one -- this entry only matters if someone relocates it.
.mtengine-ide/

# tools/mtcaps/'s own bytecode cache -- non-deterministic (tagged by whatever
# interpreter ran it, e.g. cpython-314) and regenerated for free on next run.
# Got committed 5 times across the capabilities programme by plain `git add
# -A` after a local mtcaps.py/pytest run; every one of those commits was an
# app's build (or a dev running the tests) writing inside this checkout --
# exactly what THE RULE above this file's own repo forbids.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Windows dependency archives that platform/Windows/build-*.ps1 now rebuild.
# These were tracked prebuilts (~92 MB) that nothing on Windows could
# regenerate, while macOS and Linux had build-sdl3.sh and friends all along.
# build-deps.ps1 stages them into the caps libs directory outside the
# checkout; the paths below are only where a pre-existing checkout still has
# copies. SDL2.lib/SDL2main.lib stay TRACKED: nothing here links them and no
# build-sdl2.ps1 exists yet, so untracking them could break a sibling app.
platform/Windows/libs/*/*/SDL3.lib
platform/Windows/libs/*/*/libuv.lib
platform/Windows/libs/*/*/uSockets.lib
platform/Windows/libs/*/*/freetype.lib
other/lib/uSockets.vs/**/uSockets.lib

# --- Build artifacts: never commit (compiler/linker/IDE outputs) -------------
# These rules lived only in the PUBLIC repo (16f4088) while this one tracked
# 289 MB of Visual Studio precompiled headers, IDE caches and Debug prebuilts.
# Restored here 2026-09-03 together with the deletion of that content, so both
# trees now say the same thing and the export no longer needs a frozen copy of
# someone else's .gitignore.
*.obj
*.pdb
*.ipch
*.suo
*.idb
*.vsidx
*.exp
*.ilk
*.tlog
*.iobj
*.ipdb
*.res
*.pch
*.dSYM/
*.user
**/.vs/

# freetype's autotools output from a local ./configure run. The engine builds
# freetype through CMake and platform/Windows/build-freetype.ps1; nothing reads
# these, and config.log/config.status embed the absolute paths of whichever
# machine ran configure.
other/lib/freetype-2.10.4/builds/unix/config.log
other/lib/freetype-2.10.4/builds/unix/config.status
other/lib/freetype-2.10.4/builds/unix/unix-def.mk
